From: reelee81 <reelee81@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:11:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 12/18] pdf: Skip the zero-weight corner when evaluating
 sampled functions.

interpolate_sample() reads both neighbouring samples in every input
dimension, so a sampled function with m inputs reads 2^m samples for
each evaluation, even where an input lies exactly on a sample or the
dimension has a single sample, and the second corner gets the weight
zero. A page with sixteen shadings using an 18-input function (pdf.js
test axial_shading_many_inputs.pdf) took about 1.8 s to draw on a
desktop computer. When the fractional part of an input is exactly zero,
the second corner is now skipped. The result is the same value bit for
bit; a NaN fraction still takes both corners.

Modified for ReePDF on 2026-11-12.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 source/pdf/pdf-function.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/source/pdf/pdf-function.c b/source/pdf/pdf-function.c
index 02f06d1..5765cfe 100644
--- a/source/pdf/pdf-function.c
+++ b/source/pdf/pdf-function.c
@@ -1088,11 +1088,15 @@ interpolate_sample(pdf_function_sa *func, int *scale, int *e0, int *e1, float *e
 	if (dim == 0)
 	{
 		a = func->samples[idx0];
+		if (efrac[dim] == 0)
+			return a;
 		b = func->samples[idx1];
 	}
 	else
 	{
 		a = interpolate_sample(func, scale, e0, e1, efrac, dim - 1, idx0);
+		if (efrac[dim] == 0)
+			return a;
 		b = interpolate_sample(func, scale, e0, e1, efrac, dim - 1, idx1);
 	}
 
